Ryoku

Ryoku is distributed under the GNU General Public License version 3.

Ryoku's alpha series (through v0.1.1-alpha) began as a fork of Omarchy, created by
David Heinemeier Hansson and contributors (https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy).
The beta tree was pruned and rebuilt from an empty root; its installer, shell,
theming, boot art, tooling and system definition are Ryoku's own.

The Ryoku shell is an original, custom shell. Its frame-blob rendering (the
Ryoku.Blobs QML/C++ plugin) and parts of its animation and motion curves are
adapted from the Caelestia shell:

  https://github.com/caelestia-dots/shell

Caelestia is released under the GNU General Public License version 3; the adapted
portions remain under that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

Portions of the Ryoku display configuration UI are adapted from
DankMaterialShell, created by Avenge Media LLC and contributors:

  https://github.com/AvengeMedia/DankMaterialShell

DankMaterialShell is released under the MIT License. Copyright (c) 2025
Avenge Media LLC.

The configurable frame-bar layout, bounded rail/widget catalogue organisation,
and Bar Studio workflow are adapted from OkShell by John Oberhauser:

 https://github.com/JohnOberhauser/OkShell/tree/87a9d09d923163cd03f396a395be3bb02b335b6e

OkShell is released under the GNU General Public License version 3. The adapted
frame-bar areas remain GPL-3.0-compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.


The Stage Editor (ryoku/shell/quickshell/stage/) is based on the Edit Mode of
ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx), which builds on ii-vynx by
vaguesyntax (https://github.com/vaguesyntax/ii-vynx) and end-4's
illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland). It is a modified
version: Ryoku runs it over its own desktop, widgets, wallpaper, visualizer and
depth services, gives it the Ryoku look, and leaves out its lock screen, bar and
dock editing. ii-p3drovfx is released under the GNU General Public License
version 3; the ported portions keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

The Stage Editor's symbolic icons (ryoku/shell/quickshell/stage/assets/icons/)
come from end-4's illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland) by
way of iNiR by snowarch (https://github.com/snowarch/inir). They are released
under the GNU General Public License version 3 and keep that license and their
authors' credit; PROVENANCE.txt in that folder lists them.

The ii dock design (ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/modules/dock/ii/) is based on
the dock of the same shell, ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX, Scrimas and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx), itself descended from end-4's
illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland). It is a modified
version: Ryoku reads and writes its options through its own shared dock store
and stage configuration, takes its global state, theme, window data and
keybinds from the Ryoku shell and window-manager seams, and adds the store
bridge that keeps the design in sync with the other dock designs. It is
released under the GNU General Public License version 3; the ported portions
keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

Kit's Dev tools, Commands, Typing test and Phone tabs
(ryoku/shell/quickshell/keys/) and the phone module behind the Phone tab
(ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/modules/phone/) are based on the cheatsheet pages,
phone services and Phone sidebar of ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx). They are modified versions: Ryoku
draws them in its own look inside Kit and the Hub, keeps only the developer
tools its users reach for, generates random values from /dev/urandom instead of
Math.random, runs the phone services in the shell and lets Kit drive them over
IPC, reads Android manifests with its own parser instead of pyaxmlparser, uses
scrcpy for the phone webcam and microphone instead of DroidCam, installs the
phone packages on demand, and routes window placement through the
window-manager seam. They are released under the GNU General Public License
version 3; the ported portions keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

The word lists and key sounds under ryoku/shell/quickshell/keys/typing/assets/
come from Monkeytype (https://github.com/monkeytypegame/monkeytype), released
under the GNU General Public License version 3 only; the pinned upstream commit
is recorded in the manifests beside them.

The built-in desktop sounds under ryoku/shell/ipc/assets/ come from the
freedesktop.org sound theme, renamed after the event they play for. Per that
theme's credits: the notification sound (message-new-instant) is by Damien
Sandras, GNU General Public License version 2 or later; battery-full (complete)
is by Dr. Richard Boulanger et al, Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported;
the shutter (camera-shutter) is by freesound user horsthorstensen and
volume-change (audio-volume-change) by Lucas McCallister, both Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike; the warning, plug, unplug and device sounds
(dialog-warning, power-plug, power-unplug, device-added, device-removed) are by
Ivica Bukvic, Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike.

The Redux dictation adapter (ryoku/hub/backend/assets/redux/adapter.py) is
voxtype-redux-adapter v0.2.0 by Sipper1236
(https://github.com/Sipper1236/voxtype-redux-adapter), shipped unmodified
inside ryoku-hub and installed with the Redux model from the Hub's Dictation
page. It is released under the MIT License, which ships beside it and in the
notice the installer writes; that license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3
distribution. The installer builds parakeet.cpp by the parakeet.cpp authors
(https://github.com/mudler/parakeet.cpp, MIT) with its ggml submodule on the
user's machine and downloads Moondream's Parakeet Redux weights, derived from
NVIDIA Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (CC-BY-4.0); neither ships in a Ryoku package.

The default Vesktop and Equibop theme
(ryoku/apps/vesktop/themes/ryoku-discord.theme.css) is ryoku.theme.css v3.0.0
from ryoku-discord by Ron (niqqudim, https://github.com/niqqudim/ryoku-discord)
at commit d9a0f2d, shipped unmodified under a Ryoku file name so it never
replaces a copy a user installed by hand. It builds on refact0r's midnight and
system24 Discord themes
(https://github.com/refact0r/midnight-discord and
https://github.com/refact0r/system24, both MIT). The client loads the rest of
its styles, fonts and artwork from the upstream site when the theme is enabled;
none of that ships in a Ryoku package. It is released under the MIT License,
which ships unchanged beside it and is installed as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-desktop/LICENSE.ryoku-discord; that license is
compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

The Lithic Gio icons (docs/lithic/library/go/symbols/) are Material Symbols by
Google (https://github.com/google/material-design-icons), the Sharp style at
weight 400, taken from the official SVGs at commit 10ecc147. They are modified
versions: the path data is converted to IconVG and the shapes are otherwise
unchanged. They are released under the Apache License 2.0, which ships
unchanged beside them with a NOTICE; that license is compatible with Ryoku's
GPL-3 distribution. The Rashin app draws them, and the ryoku-rashin package
installs that license as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.material-symbols.

The Lithic type (docs/lithic/library/go/fonts/) is Geologica by Monokrom,
Sindre Bremnes and Frode Helland (https://github.com/googlefonts/geologica)
and Martian Mono by Roman Shamin and Evil Martians
(https://github.com/evilmartians/mono), taken from the Google Fonts builds at
commits a60a77e1 and c8bba5c4. They are modified versions: each variable font
is cut into static instances, Geologica at full sharpness in Regular, Medium
and Bold and Martian Mono at its normal width in Regular and Bold, and renamed
to one family each; the glyphs are otherwise unchanged. They are released
under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, which ships unchanged beside them and
allows bundling the fonts with software under any license, so it is
compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution. The Rashin app and the compiled
Lithic QML module embed them. The ryoku-rashin package installs those licenses
as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.geologica and LICENSE.martian-mono,
and the ryoku-desktop package, which ships the Lithic module, installs them as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-desktop/LICENSE.geologica and LICENSE.martian-mono.

The Game Bar's Discord Voice widget
(ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/modules/gamebar/discordVoice/ and
ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/services/DiscordVoice.qml) is based on the Discord
Voice overlay widget, its RPC bridge and its Vencord companion from
ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx, compared at commit 78e333e), where
the widget is credited to xephy and the end4-pC contributors and its Discord RPC
approach to dms-discord-widget by PandorasFox
(https://github.com/PandorasFox/dms-discord-widget). It is a modified version:
Ryoku draws it as a Game Bar card in its own look, renames the companion and its
socket, and leaves out the bar widget and the install path for the official
client with Vencord. The rest of the Game Bar follows the layout of end-4's
illogical-impulse overlay (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland) without
copying its code. ii-p3drovfx is released under the GNU General Public License
version 3; the ported portions keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/, internal/ask/fence.go
and the memory tools in internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from Hermes
Agent by Nous Research (https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent) at commit
fe664929: the threat patterns memory is scanned for, the fence around recalled
memory and its streaming scrubber, the trivial-prompt gate, the secret
scrubber's token shape, the learner's grounding rule and the remember and
past-conversation search wording. It is a modified version: the patterns are
rewritten for RE2 with Rashin's paths and ids, the fence uses Rashin's tags,
and the prompts are worded for one-line facts. It is released under the MIT
License, whose text ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries with the copyright line and the
ryoku-rashin package installs as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that
license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory and ranking (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/,
internal/rank/rerank.go and internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from
OpenClaw by the OpenClaw Foundation (https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw) at
commit 3978ab10: the recall-intent patterns, the transcript filters, the checks
on the learner's operations, the untrusted-notes sentence and caps in recall,
maximal marginal relevance reranking and parts of the memory tool descriptions.
It is a modified version: ported to Go with RE2 patterns, using Rashin's fence
and markers, and reranking over Rashin's tokens. It is released under the MIT
License, whose text ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries with the copyright line and the
ryoku-rashin package installs as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that
license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory and ranking (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/,
internal/rank/rerank.go and internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from
Hindsight by Vectorize AI (https://github.com/vectorize-io/hindsight) at commit
7cec128b: the recency boost, the budget for recalled facts, the merge-or-keep
rule, the defences against invented ids, the fact extraction, consolidation and
reading rules in the prompts and parts of the remember and forget tool
descriptions. It is a modified version: budgets count characters, the
merge-or-keep rule is checked in Go rather than by a model, and the prompts are
worded for one-line facts. It is released under the MIT License, whose text
ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries with the copyright line and the ryoku-rashin
package installs as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that license is
compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/) are adapted from
nanobot by Xubin Ren and the nanobot contributors
(https://github.com/HKUDS/nanobot) at commit 9f7ae6fb: how the card shares its
room between old and new facts, the patterns that strip a model's reasoning
blocks and the archive and dream consolidation wording in the learner's
prompts. It is a modified version: the card counts characters and works on
lines, the patterns are rewritten for RE2, and the working-state handoff is
left out. It is released under the MIT License, whose text ryoku/rashin/NOTICE
carries with the copyright line and the ryoku-rashin package installs as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that license is compatible with
Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/ and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from PicoClaw by the PicoClaw
contributors (https://github.com/sipeed/picoclaw) at commit bbf6893c, which
builds on nanobot: the recent-span syntax for past-conversation search, the
shorter prompt the learner retries with after a reply that is not valid JSON
and the note that summaries are approximate. It is a modified version: the
pattern is compiled once and the learner has two rungs with no raw fallback. It
is released under the MIT License, whose text ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries with
the copyright line and the ryoku-rashin package installs as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that license is compatible with
Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/ and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from Engram by Alan Buscaglia
(https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/engram) at commit a5d4fa7b: the
<private> scrubber, the normalisation that finds duplicate facts, the result
counts, forget by id and bookkeeping rules in the memory tool descriptions and
the rule that a failed read is not evidence of absence. It is a modified
version: whitespace and word-end punctuation are handled differently and the
wording is Rashin's own; Rashin does not use the Engram name or branding. It is
released under the MIT License, whose text ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries with the
copyright line and the ryoku-rashin package installs as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE; that license is compatible with
Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/ and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from OpenAI Codex by OpenAI
(https://github.com/openai/codex) at commit b75ec48a: the phase 1 and phase 2
memory templates in the learner's prompts, the phase 1 output schema, how a
conversation is selected and stripped of what the harness injected, the shape
of the card, the ad hoc note wording of the remember tool and the rule that
memory is history, not proof. It is a modified version: the output is Rashin's
JSON fact operations, the personal scope is a profile rather than task history
and consolidation is a single call. It is released under the Apache License
2.0; ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries Codex's NOTICE ("OpenAI Codex / Copyright 2025
OpenAI"), and the ryoku-rashin package installs the license as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0. That license is compatible
with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/ and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from mem0 by Taranjeet Singh and the
mem0ai/mem0 contributors (https://github.com/mem0ai/mem0) at commit f1791daa:
the learner's extraction, update and import prompts, the facts-list answer and
fenced JSON parsing, short ids for existing facts, the noise filters for
conversation text and the memory-triage tests and forget wording in the memory
tool descriptions. It is a modified version: each fact carries its scope and
sensitivity, a delete becomes a supersede, and the chat-platform filters are
left out. The forget wording comes from mem0's Hermes plugin, which also
carries Nous Research's MIT notice. It is released under the Apache License
2.0; ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries the copyright line, and the ryoku-rashin
package installs the license as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0. That license is compatible
with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/ and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from Letta by the Letta authors
(https://github.com/letta-ai/letta) at commit 56ba9c25, the last commit with
the server's source: the read-only memory block and its metadata line in
recall, the consolidation principles in the learner's card prompt and the
self-contained-fact and five-result rules in the memory tool descriptions. It
is a modified version: one read-only block holds the card about the user, the
metadata counts facts and conversations only, and the wording is Rashin's own.
It is released under the Apache License 2.0; ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries the
copyright line, and the ryoku-rashin package installs the license as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0. That license is compatible
with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Parts of Rashin's memory (ryoku/rashin/internal/memory/prompts.go and
internal/brief/memorytools.go) are adapted from Letta Code by the Letta authors
(https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-code) at commit 417a8b7b: the reflection and
core-memory maintenance wording in the learner's prompts and the Memory tool's
rule to open the matching memory rather than work from assumptions. It is a
modified version: there are no files, git or procedures, a stale fact is
superseded rather than archived, and the examples are new. It is released under
the Apache License 2.0; ryoku/rashin/NOTICE carries the copyright line, and the
ryoku-rashin package installs the license as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0. That license is compatible
with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

